design "Seatpost Number Bracket"
note "A screwless race-number holder that fits round seatposts, D-shaped aero posts and the Giant Propel's, held on the way rear lights are. The bracket is a plain block that sits behind the post on a strip of rubber you cut, with a jaw behind it and an arm either side. The block's front face is the seat: flat, with a shallow groove down its middle, so a flat-backed post lies flat on the face and a round post nests in the groove. Each arm swings on a boss beside the block and prints in place round it. One EPDM O-ring's ends drop into channels in the backs of the arms' heads (the end loops over the head's top and under its bottom and slides in from the side), and it wraps the post's front with a strand high and one low, so its pull presses the seat onto the post's back and swings each arm in until its rubber-faced finger grips the post's side: the holder is held at three places whatever the post's shape. The number's pointed end slides into a slot in the jaw only 0.2 mm wider than the number and nests in a V pocket of its own outline there, which holds it at one tilt, its tail 15° above level. A slim one-piece number clip keeps it there, hugging the jaw: its round pin, which fills the number's centre hole (where the old bolt went), pushes through one cheek, the number and the other cheek, and its leg runs under the jaw and clicks up behind the far cheek's bottom edge. No pull on its head and no knock on its pin takes it out: press the hook's nose, past the far cheek, down with a fingertip and pull the clip out by the small tab under it."
note "Everything about the bracket and its clip is drawn in the seatpost's own frame and leaned back with it (rotate: (-tilt, 0, 0)). The number's outline is turned about its centre hole by tilt plus rise, so its tail rises rise degrees above level."
note "v4, from the printed v3 and its side-arm rework: the pin spans the whole slot and goes on into the far cheek, and every overhang prints without supports (the pin and its holes are teardrops)."
note "v5: the number tilts up 30°, the bracket is 20 mm tall (the O-ring's double hooks as short as a 3.53 mm cord allows) and its seat fits a 20 mm flat back. The jaw is a plain block standing on the bed with the tilted pocket cut through it."
note "v6: the tilt adjusts by hand and is firm at each click: the number sits in a dial (a third small print) that turns about its centre hole on a stub off the far cheek and is locked by a sprung latch whose wedge teeth take up all play. The centre hole sits 3.2 mm higher so the dial's teeth fit under it in the same 20 mm height."
note "v7: the tilt is changed by pulling one big arrow-shaped tab straight out, about 7.5 N, rather than pressing a small tab sideways with about 27 N. The arrow points the way to pull, the latch springs back into the next click on its own, and a requirement keeps the pull light."
note "v8: pulling v7's latch let the dial drop off its stub, because the latch was all that held it on. Now the dial goes on a keyed stub through a keyhole that only lines up 35° past the top click, which the number cannot reach (its corner meets the seatpost first), and there is nothing to pull: a spring blade outside the far cheek presses a V finger through a window into three grooves under the dial, preloaded so there is no play."
note "v9, from the printed v8, which snapped: nothing bends and nothing turns. The number is held at the top tilt only, its point in a V pocket cut through a solid jaw, in a slot 0.2 mm wider than it, and a separate snap pin goes through the jaw and the number. The saddle is 5.3 mm thick behind its seat with nothing cut into it."
note "v10: fits any seatpost, and the number sits flatter. The middle's seat is a 17.5 mm radius curve with a TPU pad shaped to the post in it (the one part that changes with the post), and the double hooks are on two arms that clip onto bosses either side and swing in or out to follow the post, the way you sketched it. Each arm's clip is a C that snaps over its boss and a bead in it rides in a groove round the boss, so the arm stays at its height. The clip's opening faces the neck that holds its boss, forward and in, so the O-ring's pull always presses the clip onto the back of its boss rather than out through the opening. The number's tail rises 15° instead of 30°, and its pin sits 2 mm higher so the jaw stays thick under the flatter number. The O-ring is a plain 3.5 mm metric one sized to the post (no joined double band was for sale)."
note "v11, from the printed v10 arm: each arm is one solid piece that stands flat on the bed, with no groove under it to bridge, and its body runs full width from the clip to the head, where v10's joined the clip by a sliver. The O-ring no longer threads through narrow gaps between prongs: each end loops over the head and slides into a rounded channel in its back, which flares at the top and bottom so the cord bends gently, and the pull holds it three quarters of a cord deep. The clip slides down over its boss from the top and clicks into a groove near the boss's top, about 24 N on and off; its opening is 1.4 mm narrower than the boss, so it cannot come off sideways however the arm is knocked. The arms are now a right and a left, each the other's mirror image, so both slide on from the top."
note "v12, from your ask for a number clip that is easier to use, clicks in stronger and is easy to take out: v11's snap pin is now a one-piece clip. Its square pin is solid rather than split, its head stands 7 mm out of the near cheek to push and pull it by, and its leg runs under the jaw's flat bottom and clicks up behind the far cheek's bottom edge. Its spine and leg bend together, so it pushes in with about 9 N and takes about 25 N to pull out, where the pin let go at about 7 N; pressing the leg's sloped end, below the far cheek, down and in lets it go and pushes it out. Latched, its catch stays bent a quarter of a millimetre, so the clip sits tight. The bracket is v11's, unchanged, so only the clip needs printing."
note "v13, from your printed v11 and v12, which came apart too easily: both clips are now far tighter. The arms print in place, standing on the bed round their bosses with 0.4 mm of room all round: a groove 1.2 mm deep runs round each boss's middle and a ring inside the arm's clip runs in it, reaching 0.8 mm under the boss's edge, so an arm swings freely but cannot be pulled off up, down or sideways; one firm turn after printing breaks it free. The number clip has a catch 1.3 mm deep (v12's was 0.8) with a 70° face, which no pull on the head can open (it would take about 84 N), on a leg twice as long so it still bends gently, and its pin runs on 4 mm past the far cheek. To take it out, press the tab past the catch down (about 4 N) and push it toward the jaw. v12's thin spine would have started to bend for good if its head was pulled hard (from about 20 N); this one is thick enough for 40 N. Bracket, arms and clip all need reprinting; the pad is unchanged."
note "v14, from your asks for a clip that is less in the way but still easy to pull out, with a round pin that fits the number's hole, and a better clip altogether: the clip is rethought as a slim C that hugs the jaw. It is about half v13's size (14 × 23 mm against 27 × 21, 0.7 g) and nothing on it stands more than 4 mm out from the jaw, where v13's head stood out 12 mm and its tab 7. The pin is round, 4.8 mm for the number's 5.2 mm hole, cut flat only along the side it prints on, with a pointed tip that finds the hole; the jaw's hole is round to match, with a small flat in its top so it prints clean. The catch is as steep as v13's (70°) and 1 mm deep, so no pull on the clip's head and no knock on its pin opens it. To take it out, press the hook's sloped nose down with a fingertip (about 8 N) and pull the clip out by the tab under the jaw, which takes almost nothing; on a smooth print a firm pull on the tab alone peels it off. Ideas tried in simulation and dropped: a catch shallow enough to pull straight off (it would feel loose again), a twist lock (a leg brushing it could turn it), and a latch built into the jaw (too short to bend safely). The bracket's only change is the round hole, so the bracket with its arms and the clip need reprinting; the pad is unchanged."
note "v15, from your asks to improve the whole holder, make it easier to print and more compact, keep it fitting different seatposts, and drop the printed pad for rubber you cut yourself: the swinging arms and the TPU pad are gone, and the bracket is one solid print 30 mm wide, 20.5 mm deep and 20 mm tall (v14 with its arms was 50 × 34 × 20), about 6 g. The arms added width, two more prints and a joint each side (the one that came apart on v11), and the print-in-place gaps round their pivots were the hardest thing to print. The O-ring's ends now drop into channels in the bracket's own shoulders, level with the post's back, and its strands run forward along the post's sides. The seat is the bracket's flat front face with a shallow groove down its middle, lined with a strip of rubber about 2 mm thick that you cut 21 × 20 mm and stick in: a flat-backed post like the Propel's lies flat on the face either side of the groove, and a round post from 25.4 to 31.6 mm nests in the groove on two lines, like a rod in a V block, so neither can rock, and the same bracket and strip fit them all. Only the O-ring's size follows the post, and it is longer than before because it now goes the whole way round: 56.74 × 3.53 mm for the Propel. The number clip is v14's, unchanged. Two prints, both ASA, standing or lying flat on the bed with no supports and no gaps to keep open."
note "v16, from your call to keep the two arms so it locks onto the post, with a better design: v15's solid bracket touched the post only at its seat, and on the Propel's flat back a sideways push of about 370 g on the number's tail was enough to start it rocking. The arms are back, on v15's plain block: the O-ring's pull swings each one in until its finger grips the post's side, so the holder is held at the seat and a finger each side, and the fingers must be dragged along the post before the bracket can turn or twist. On the Propel that takes about 590 g on the tail, and the number itself folds over at about 310 g, so a knock bends the number before it moves the bracket. What is better than v14's arms: the O-ring pulls 6 mm outboard of each pivot (v14: 3.4) and is stretched 40 % (v14: 35 %), so each finger presses on with about 26 N where v14's pressed with about 20; each finger is faced with a small patch of rubber, so it grips where bare plastic slid and keeps the plastic off the post's paint; the pivots sit 1 mm closer in and the block is a plain 17.4 mm one with no cuts for the clips; and there is still no printed pad: the flat seat with its groove and the rubber you cut are v15's. As printed it is 50 mm wide, 31 mm deep and 20 mm tall (47 mm wide on the Propel). The O-ring is v14's size again, 44 × 3.5 mm for the Propel, since it only goes from arm to arm round the post's front; v15's 56.74 mm one does not fit. Nothing held on by a rubber ring is as rigid as a bolted clamp: a deliberate push of about 460 g up or down on the tail still nods it, and it springs back. The number clip is v14's, unchanged. Two prints, both ASA: the bracket with both arms in place round their bosses, and the clip; and three bits of rubber to cut."

brief {
  purpose: "Hold a race number behind a seatpost with no screws: round posts, D-shaped aero posts and the Giant Propel's"
  setting: "Road and triathlon racing: fitted and taken off by hand at the bike, ridden in rain and sun"
  priorities: ["fits every common seatpost shape: a seat that takes flat backs and round posts on one strip of rubber you cut, two arms that swing to the post's sides, and an O-ring sized to the post", "a gentle tilt: the number's tail 15° above level", "strong: solid prints with no thin springs or slits to snap", "the number never moves: its point nests in a pocket at one fixed tilt and a pin goes through it", "locks onto the post: held at the seat and by a rubber-faced finger each side, so a knock folds the number before it moves the bracket", "the arms cannot come off: printed in place round their bosses", "a slim number clip that hugs the jaw, clicks in, stays in until you press its nose, and then pulls out easily by its tab", "a round pin that fills the number's centre hole", "the number is tight in its slot: 0.2 mm of room", "compact: no bigger than it must be to reach round the post", "easy to print: standing on the bed, no supports, the arms printed in place", "fits by hand with one O-ring", "does not rock or turn on the post"]
  look: "Black, small, tucked in behind the post"

  temp: -5C .. 50C
  outdoor: true
  exposure: ["sunlight", "rain", "vibration", "impact"]

  process: "FDM print"
  material: "ASA"
  quantity: 1

  use gust "Holds the number in a 70 km/h crosswind gust" { on: bracket }
  use knock "Survives a leg or hand knocking the number's tail, and the number does not tilt" { on: bracket }
  use bumps "Stays put over potholes (about 12 g)" { on: bracket }
  use fit "Goes on and comes off by hand, no tools, on any of the seatposts" { on: bracket }

  given tilt = 16.5 "How far the seatpost leans back from vertical, degrees (73.5° seat angle)" assumed
  given plate_l = 122mm "Number length, read from the photo" assumed
  given plate_h = 69mm "Number height, read from the photo" assumed
  given plate_t = 1mm "Number thickness" assumed
  given taper_l = 10mm "Length of the number's pointed end, read from the photo" assumed
  given tip_h = 6mm "Flat at the very point (under the old bracket's arm in the photo)" assumed
  given hole_c = 4.5mm "From the point to the centre hole (under the old bracket's washer in the photo)" assumed
  given hole_cd = 5.2mm "Centre hole, which takes the old bracket's M5 bolt" assumed
  given hole_d = 3.5mm "The other holes, read from the photo" assumed
  given hole_in = 11.5mm "From the point to the corner holes, read from the photo" assumed
  given hole_z = 19mm "Corner holes above and below the middle, read from the photo" assumed

  reference photo "The number on its old bolted bracket" { notes: "Race Result plate 4004, tag-shaped with a blunt pointed end; the old bracket's M5 bolt goes through the centre hole at the point, two more holes 38 mm apart about 7 mm behind it, two along the top edge; yellow stripe at the tail, timing transponder sticker across the top half, 4004 in heavy black digits with a small leading 4" }
  reference propel "Giant Propel seatpost" { url: "https://www.giant-bicycles.com/", notes: "Giant publishes no section drawing of the aero seatpost: about 20 mm wide and 36 mm deep with a flat back, until it is measured" }
  reference lights "How rear lights hold on with one band" { notes: "dan's photo of a rear light: one rubber loop wraps the post with a strand high and a strand low, and each end hooks onto a double hook on the light's side, the upper hook turned up and the lower one down, so the band cannot rock the light about it. Garmin Varia's mount adds a rubber shim shaped to the post (round or aero), keyed into the bracket; riders on D-shaped posts report it turning unless the shim fits the post" }
  reference rings_rs "RS PRO EPDM O-rings, 3.5 mm cord" { url: "https://au.rs-online.com/web/p/gaskets-o-rings/2587581", notes: "44 × 3.5 mm (stock no. 258-7581), sold in bags of 20; 41 × 3.5 is 258-7572 and 37.5 × 3.5 is 258-7543. EPDM is the rubber that lasts outdoors" }
  reference rings_autopro "Champion metric O-rings at Autopro, 3.5 mm cord" { url: "https://autopro.com.au/ap/Autopro-Category/Brands/Champion-Packs/Champion-44mm-x-3-5mm-Metric-O-Rings-Sold-Individually---C408-30/p/SP40374", notes: "Nitrile, sold one at a time, click and collect: 44 mm (C408-30) $12.99, 41 mm (C408-29) and 36 mm (C408-27) $1.49 each. Nitrile cracks in sunlight, so swap it each season" }

  assume "The number is polypropylene, about 30 MPa yield"
  assume "Painted carbon post: EPDM grips it with μ 0.8 dry, 0.5 wet; a strip of rubber sheet or inner tube about 0.5 wet; bare ASA only about 0.3"
}

// ── The seatpost ───────────────────────────────────────
param post_w   = 20mm   range 18 .. 32  "Seatpost width, side to side at its widest"
param post_d   = 36mm   range 18 .. 45  "Seatpost depth, front to back (the width again for a round post)"
param tail_w   = 20mm   range 0 .. 24   "Width across the post's back, corner to corner: 0 for a round post"
param tail_r   = 1.5mm  range 0 .. 4    "Radius of the post's back corners"

// ── The bracket and its rubber ────────────────────────
param body_h   = 20mm   range 20 .. 30  "Height along the post of the bracket and its jaw"
param rubber_t = 2mm    range 1 .. 3    "Thickness of the rubber strip you cut for the seat (an old inner tube doubled is about 2 mm)"

param finger_t = 1mm    range 0.5 .. 2  "Thickness of the rubber patch on each arm's finger (one layer of inner tube is about 1 mm)"

// ── The O-ring and its channels ────────────────────────
param oring_id = 44mm   range 30 .. 70  "O-ring inside diameter: the seatpost choice sets the size that stretches about 40 % from arm to arm round the post's front"
param oring_cs = 3.5mm  range 3 .. 3.53 "O-ring cord (3.5 mm metric, or 3.53 mm)"
param prong_d  = 5.5mm  range 5.25 .. 6 "The bend the O-ring makes over the top and bottom of each channel, as a diameter (at least 1.5 cords, so it does not wear through)"

// ── The jaw and its number clip ────────────────────────
param pivot_y  = 16mm   range 14 .. 24  "From the bracket's front face back to the number's centre hole, where the pin goes"
param rise     = 15     range 0 .. 30   "How far the number's tail rises above level, degrees: the jaw's pocket holds it there and nowhere else (reprint to change it)"
param fit_gap  = 0.2mm  range 0.1 .. 0.6 "Room for the number in its slot, both sides together: 0.2 holds it tight. If your printer prints narrow slots under size and the number will not go in, try 0.3"
param cheek    = 3mm    range 2.4 .. 4  "Jaw cheek thickness either side of the number"
param pin_d    = 4.8mm  range 4 .. 5    "The number clip's round pin: 0.4 mm smaller than the number's centre hole (5.2 mm), so it fills it"

// ── Duty ───────────────────────────────────────────────
param gust     = 70kmh  range 30kmh .. 120kmh "Crosswind gust"
param knock    = 25N    range 5 .. 60   "A leg or hand knocking the number's tail"
param bump_g   = 12     range 3 .. 25   "Pothole shock, in g"

let rubber_mu = 0.5                                  // rubber on wet painted carbon
let ct       = cos(tilt)
let st       = sin(tilt)
let post_ax  = (0, st, ct)

// ── The post's section: a round nose, straight sides and two round back corners (or just a circle) ──
// x across, y back from the bracket's front face. The seat is that flat face with a shallow groove down its middle, lined
// with a rubber strip: a flat-backed post wider than the groove sits flat on the face either side of it, and a round
// post (or a narrow tail) nests in the groove on its two sloping sides, so neither can rock
let seat_a   = 6.4                                   // half the groove's mouth
let seat_f   = 1.5                                   // half its flat floor
let seat_v   = 20                                    // its sides' slope, degrees
let seat_g   = (seat_a - seat_f) * tan(seat_v)       // its depth
let apex_y   = seat_a * tan(seat_v)                  // where its sides would meet
let round_post = tail_w < 0.5
let Rn       = post_w / 2
let kx0      = max(tail_w / 2 - tail_r, 0)
let R1       = Rn + rubber_t                         // a round post and the rubber on it
let r1       = tail_r + rubber_t                     // a back corner and the rubber on it
// a round post touches both sides of the groove (or its mouth, if it is too big to reach in)
let ny_round = R1 * sin(seat_v) <= seat_a ? apex_y - R1 / cos(seat_v) : -sqrt(R1^2 - seat_a^2)
// a flat back that spans the groove lies on the face; a narrower one sits in the groove on its corners
let ky_flat  = kx0 >= seat_a ? -r1 : (kx0 + r1 * sin(seat_v) <= seat_a ? apex_y - kx0 * tan(seat_v) - r1 / cos(seat_v) : -sqrt(r1^2 - (seat_a - kx0)^2))
let ys       = round_post ? ny_round + post_d - Rn : ky_flat + tail_r    // the post's back
let ny       = ys - post_d + Rn                      // the nose's centre
let ky       = ys - tail_r                           // the back corners' centres
// the straight side from the nose to the back corner: its outward normal and where it leaves each
let sd       = hypot(kx0, ky - ny)
let ex       = kx0 / sd
let ey       = (ky - ny) / sd
let s0       = (Rn - tail_r) / sd
let c0       = sqrt(max(1 - s0^2, 0))
let snx      = s0 * ex + c0 * ey
let sny      = s0 * ey - c0 * ex
let tnx      = Rn * snx
let tny      = ny + Rn * sny
let tkx      = kx0 + tail_r * snx
let tky      = ky + tail_r * sny
let post_sec = round_post ? circle(post_w, at: (0, ny)) : circle(post_w, at: (0, ny)) + polygon(points: [(-tnx, tny), (tnx, tny), (tkx, tky), (-tkx, tky)]) + rect(tail_w, 2 * tail_r + 0.4, at: (0, ky - 0.2), r: max(tail_r, 0.1))

// ── The middle ─────────────────────────────────────────
// one plain block behind the post, a little narrower than a 20 mm post so the arms' pivots can sit close in. Its front
// face is the seat
let bx       = 8.7
let body_back = 7.5

// ── The bosses, their necks and the arms' clips ───────
// a boss each side on a neck that runs forward and in, neck_a below straight in, to the block's side. Each arm's clip is
// a C round its boss with its opening over the neck, and the opening is narrower than the boss, so the clip cannot come
// off sideways. The arms print in place, standing on the bed with the bracket, each clip already round its boss with clr
// of room all round: a deep groove runs round the boss's middle and a ring inside the clip runs in it, so the arm cannot
// come off up or down either. A firm turn after printing breaks it free, and from then on it swings
let px       = 14.5
let rb       = 3                                     // boss radius
let neck_a   = 25
let nw       = 3                                     // narrow, so the clip's opening can be well under the boss's width
// as far forward as the neck lets it be: the neck's front edge meets the block's side just behind its front face
let py       = (px - bx) * tan(neck_a) + nw / 2 / cos(neck_a) + 0.15
let neck_l   = (px - bx) / cos(neck_a) + nw / 2 * tan(neck_a) + 0.1    // just long enough that its end is lost in the block
let clr      = 0.4                                   // room between the boss and its clip, everywhere: enough to print in place
let ri       = rb + clr
let kw       = 1.8                                   // the clip's wall
let ro       = ri + kw
let relief   = px - ro - bx                          // room between each clip and the block's side
// every post in the seatpost choice sets its arms between the D-shaped post's -5° and the 31.6 mm post's 14°, so the
// opening is centred on the neck at th_c, about halfway, and lets the arm turn swing degrees either way of it before its
// edge meets the neck
let th_c     = 5
let swing    = 18
let mouth_c  = 180 + neck_a - th_c                   // the opening's middle, in the arm's frame
let mouth_h  = asin((nw / 2) / ri) + swing           // half its angle
let mouth_w  = 2 * ri * sin(mouth_h)                 // its width
// the groove round each boss's middle: gd deep, with a 1 mm flat between flanks that stand at 55° to the bed (1.4 up for
// each 1 in), so the boss prints without support. The ring inside the clip is the groove's own shape grown by clr, so it
// keeps clr of room from the boss everywhere yet reaches gd - clr inside the boss's edge: the arm cannot be pulled off
// without breaking it
let gd       = 1.2
let zg1      = -(2 * 1.4 * gd + 1) / 2               // the groove's lower edge; its upper one is -zg1
let k_off    = sqrt(1 + 1.4^2) - 1.4                 // grown by clr, a flank moves this much (per mm of clr) along the boss past its corner
let ring_r   = rb - gd + clr                         // the ring's inside
// the neck fills the groove under the clip's opening, so the ring stops short of it, and of where the neck swings to:
// its ends stand ring_h either side of the opening's middle
let ring_h   = asin((nw / 2) / ring_r) + swing + 5
let ch_bed   = 0.4                                   // 45° chamfers round the bosses' and the clips' bottom edges, so the first layer's squash cannot join them

// ── Each arm, in its own frame: pivot at (0, 0), pointing forward (-y), outward +x, standing on its bottom face as it
// prints ──
// one solid piece standing flat on the bed: the clip, a body running forward beside the post, and a head across its tip.
// The body's inside face is the finger, an 8 mm radius faced with a patch of rubber, that rests on the post's side about
// 4 mm in front of a flat back. The O-ring's end drops into an upright channel in the back of the head from the side,
// xu outboard of the pivot: the further out it pulls, the harder it swings the finger onto the post. The channel flares
// out round prong_d / 2 at its top and bottom, so the cord bends gently over them whichever way the post pulls it, and
// the bottom flare is a 50° cone with a rounded rim, so it prints without support
let rf       = 8
let xi       = -3.25                                 // the finger's middle: a 20 mm post and 1 mm of rubber set the arm near straight ahead
let yf       = -(py + 6)
let yft      = yf + 3                                // the finger's upper end
let ybf      = yf + 2.3                              // the head's back face: the further back, the further the O-ring is stretched
let hd       = 6.8                                   // and its depth: 1.2 mm of solid in front of the top flare
let yfront   = ybf - hd
let cord_max = 3.53                                  // the thickest cord the channels take
let ch_r     = cord_max / 2 + 0.2                    // the channel: the cord and 0.2 of room (a 3.5 mm cord fits too)
let ch_e     = 0.9                                   // its middle sits this far into the head, so the cord sits three quarters of its thickness deep
let xu       = 6
let ny_c     = ybf - ch_e
let xb       = xu - ch_r                             // the body's outside face, which runs on into the channel's inner side
let xend     = xu + ch_r + 1.2                       // the head's outer end: a 1.2 mm wall outside the channel
let rr       = prong_d / 2
let lip      = ch_r + ch_e                           // how far the O-ring's end is pulled back to get it into the channel or out
let aB       = mouth_c + mouth_h + 3.5               // where the body's inside face leaves the clip, just past its opening
let fx_t     = xi + rf - sqrt(rf^2 - (yft - yf)^2)
let fx_f     = xi + rf - sqrt(rf^2 - (yfront - yf)^2)
let arm_plan = outline([(0, 0), (ro * cos(aB), ro * sin(aB)), (fx_t, yft), arc_to((fx_f, yfront), via: (xi, yf)), (xend, yfront), (xend, ybf), (xu, ybf), (xu, ny_c), (xb, ny_c), (xb, -ri + 0.2)])
let arm_body = extrude(arm_plan, body_h, at: (0, 0, -body_h / 2))
let knuckle  = revolve(points: [(0, -body_h / 2), (ro - ch_bed, -body_h / 2), (ro, -body_h / 2 + ch_bed), (ro, body_h / 2), (0, body_h / 2)], at: (0, 0, 0), dir: Z)
let bore_s   = revolve(points: [(0, -body_h / 2 - 1), (ri + ch_bed + 1, -body_h / 2 - 1), (ri, -body_h / 2 + ch_bed), (ri, body_h / 2 + 1), (0, body_h / 2 + 1)], at: (0, 0, 0), dir: Z)
// the ring, rooted 0.5 mm into the clip's wall, runs round from one side of the neck to the other
let ring_s   = revolve(polygon(points: [(ri + 0.5, zg1 + k_off * clr - 0.7), (ring_r, zg1 + 1.4 * gd + k_off * clr), (ring_r, -zg1 - 1.4 * gd - k_off * clr), (ri + 0.5, -zg1 - k_off * clr + 0.7)]), at: (0, 0, 0), axis: Z, angle: 360 - 2 * ring_h, rot: mouth_c + ring_h)
let mouth_p  = overlap(polygon(points: [(0, 0), (2 * ro * cos(mouth_c - mouth_h), 2 * ro * sin(mouth_c - mouth_h)), (2 * ro * cos(mouth_c), 2 * ro * sin(mouth_c)), (2 * ro * cos(mouth_c + mouth_h), 2 * ro * sin(mouth_c + mouth_h))]), circle(2 * ro + 0.1))
let mouth_s  = extrude(mouth_p, body_h + 2, at: (0, 0, -body_h / 2 - 1))
// the channel, as a solid of revolution about its upright middle: straight from zb to zt, round at the top, and at the
// bottom round to 40° from upright, then a 40° cone, then a rbed round into the bed
let zt       = body_h / 2 - rr
let zb       = -body_h / 2 + rr
let rbed     = 1.2
let rc       = ch_r + rr * (1 - cos(40) + (1 - sin(40)) * tan(40))
let tb       = rbed * tan(25)
let channel_s = revolve(outline([(0, -body_h / 2 - 1), (rc + tb, -body_h / 2 - 1), (rc + tb, -body_h / 2), arc_to((rc - tb * sin(40), -body_h / 2 + tb * cos(40)), via: (rc + tb - rbed * sin(25), -body_h / 2 + rbed - rbed * cos(25))), (ch_r + rr * (1 - cos(40)), zb - rr * sin(40)), arc_to((ch_r, zb), via: (ch_r + rr * (1 - cos(20)), zb - rr * sin(20))), (ch_r, zt), arc_to((ch_r + rr, body_h / 2), via: (ch_r + rr * (1 - cos(45)), zt + rr * sin(45))), (ch_r + rr, body_h / 2 + 1), (0, body_h / 2 + 1)]), at: (xu, ny_c, 0), axis: Z)
// the flares stop at the channel's outer side, so the 1.2 mm wall outside the channel keeps its full height (the O-ring's
// strands leave forward and in, never over that wall)
let wall_s   = extrude(polygon(points: [(xu + ch_r, yfront), (xend, yfront), (xend, ybf), (xu + ch_r, ybf)]), body_h, at: (0, 0, -body_h / 2))

// ── Where each arm rests: it swings in until its finger's rubber meets the post ──
// the rubber's face is a circle of radius rf + finger_t about q in the arm's frame; turned by th about the boss it
// first meets the post's nose, a back corner or the straight side between them, whichever comes at the widest swing
let fo       = rf + finger_t
let fr       = fo + 0.05
let qx0      = xi + rf
let qn       = hypot(qx0, yf)
let aq       = atan2(yf, qx0)
let dNx      = -px
let dNy      = ny - py
let dN       = hypot(dNx, dNy)
let th_N0    = atan2(dNy, dNx) - aq + acos(clamp((qn^2 + dN^2 - (Rn + fr)^2) / (2 * qn * dN), -1, 1))
let th_N     = th_N0 - 360 * round(th_N0 / 360)
let dKx      = kx0 - px
let dKy      = ky - py
let dK       = hypot(dKx, dKy)
let th_K0    = atan2(dKy, dKx) - aq + acos(clamp((qn^2 + dK^2 - (tail_r + fr)^2) / (2 * qn * dK), -1, 1))
let th_K     = round_post ? -999 : th_K0 - 360 * round(th_K0 / 360)
let an       = atan2(sny, snx)
let c_side   = sny * ny + Rn
let th_S0    = an - aq - acos(clamp((fr + c_side - (snx * px + sny * py)) / qn, -1, 1))
let th_S1    = th_S0 - 360 * round(th_S0 / 360)
let qSx      = px + qx0 * cos(th_S1) - yf * sin(th_S1)
let qSy      = py + qx0 * sin(th_S1) + yf * cos(th_S1)
let side_l   = max(hypot(tkx - tnx, tky - tny), 0.01)
let side_t   = ((qSx - fr * snx - tnx) * (tkx - tnx) + (qSy - fr * sny - tny) * (tky - tny)) / side_l
let th_S     = round_post ? -999 : side_t < -0.01 || side_t > side_l + 0.01 ? -999 : th_S1
let th_free  = max(th_N, th_K, th_S)
let th       = clamp(th_free, th_c - swing, th_c + swing)
let on_N     = th_N >= th_K && th_N >= th_S
let on_K     = th_K > th_N && th_K >= th_S
// the finger's centre as it rests, the normal where it touches (from the post toward it) and the touching point
let qx       = px + qx0 * cos(th) - yf * sin(th)
let qy       = py + qx0 * sin(th) + yf * cos(th)
let cnx0     = on_N ? qx : on_K ? qx - kx0 : snx
let cny0     = on_N ? qy - ny : on_K ? qy - ky : sny
let cnx      = cnx0 / hypot(cnx0, cny0)
let cny      = cny0 / hypot(cnx0, cny0)
let fcx      = qx - fo * cnx
let fcy      = qy - fo * cny
// where on the finger it touches, from its middle: it must stay on the rubber, short of the patch's ends. The patch
// runs from the finger's upper end to 0.8 mm short of the head's front
let psi      = atan2(-cnx * sin(th) + cny * cos(th), cnx * cos(th) + cny * sin(th))
let psi_p0   = asin((yf - yft) / rf)
let psi_p1   = asin((yf - yfront - 0.8) / rf)
let psi_lo   = asin((yf - yft + 1) / rf)
let psi_hi   = asin((yf - yfront - 1.8) / rf)
let patch_w  = rf * (psi_p1 - psi_p0) * 0.0174533     // what to cut: the patch's width round the finger
let patch_sec = overlap(circle(2 * fo, at: (qx0, yf)) - circle(2 * rf, at: (qx0, yf)), polygon(points: [(qx0, yf), (qx0 - 3 * fo * cos(psi_p0), yf - 3 * fo * sin(psi_p0)), (qx0 - 3 * fo * cos((psi_p0 + psi_p1) / 2), yf - 3 * fo * sin((psi_p0 + psi_p1) / 2)), (qx0 - 3 * fo * cos(psi_p1), yf - 3 * fo * sin(psi_p1))]))
// the O-ring's end in arm_r's channel: the strands leave it hook_a in from straight forward in the arm's own frame
// (about 8° on the D-shaped post, 17° on the Propel's and 35° on round ones; 25° is a middle value), so they bend over
// the flares' rounds about this far in front of the channel's middle. In the bracket's frame; arm_l's is its mirror image
let hook_a   = 25
let hk_x     = xu - (ch_r + rr) * sin(hook_a)
let hk_y     = ny_c - (ch_r + rr) * cos(hook_a)
let hRx      = px + hk_x * cos(th) - hk_y * sin(th)
let hRy      = py + hk_x * sin(th) + hk_y * cos(th)
// the channel's middle, through which the O-ring's pull on the arm passes
let nRx      = px + xu * cos(th) - ny_c * sin(th)
let nRy      = py + xu * sin(th) + ny_c * cos(th)

part post {
  label: "Seatpost"
  material: "Carbon fiber"
  finish: "gloss"
  color: #17181b
  group: "Bike"
  process: false
  rotate: (-tilt, 0, 0)
  body extrude(post_sec, 150, at: (0, 0, -70))
}

// ── The jaw ────────────────────────────────────────────
// the number turns about its centre hole H: by the post's lean and on by rise, so its tail rises rise degrees above
// level; its point J sits hole_c in front of H. H sits above the bracket's middle, so the jaw runs on under the number's
// lower edge as far back as the jaw goes
let H_z      = 3.5
let ja       = tilt + rise
let ca       = cos(ja)
let sa       = sin(ja)
let jaw_y    = pivot_y - hole_c * ca
let J        = (0, jaw_y, H_z - hole_c * sa)
// where J ends up in the world once everything leans back
let J_w      = (0, jaw_y * ct + J.z * st, -jaw_y * st + J.z * ct)
// the way the number runs back, in the world
let num_dir  = (0, cos(rise), sin(rise))
// tilted up, the number's top front corner swings forward toward the post: where it ends up, behind the bracket's front face
let corner_y = pivot_y + (taper_l - hole_c) * ca - plate_h / 2 * sa
let jaw_front = body_back - 1
let cheek_back = pivot_y + 4.5
let jaw_r    = 3
let jaw_sec  = outline([(jaw_front, -body_h / 2), (cheek_back, -body_h / 2), (cheek_back, body_h / 2 - jaw_r), arc_to((cheek_back - jaw_r, body_h / 2), via: (cheek_back - jaw_r + 0.7071 * jaw_r, body_h / 2 - jaw_r + 0.7071 * jaw_r)), (jaw_front, body_h / 2)])
let slot     = plate_t + fit_gap                     // the number's slot through the jaw
let jaw_w    = slot + 2 * cheek                      // the near cheek stands from slot / 2 to jaw_w / 2, the far one the other side
// the number's outline: its point at (0, 0), running back along +u, up +v; and the same about its centre hole
let tag      = polygon(points: [(0, -tip_h / 2), (taper_l, -plate_h / 2), (plate_l, -plate_h / 2), (plate_l, plate_h / 2), (taper_l, plate_h / 2), (0, tip_h / 2)], r: 3)
let tag_h    = polygon(points: [(-hole_c, -tip_h / 2), (taper_l - hole_c, -plate_h / 2), (plate_l - hole_c, -plate_h / 2), (plate_l - hole_c, plate_h / 2), (taper_l - hole_c, plate_h / 2), (-hole_c, tip_h / 2)], r: 3)
let pocket   = rotate(offset(tag_h, fit_gap / 2), ja)
// where the number's upper edge leaves the top of the jaw, and how far the jaw runs on under its lower edge at the back
let edge_y   = taper_l * ca - (plate_h - tip_h) / 2 * sa
let edge_z   = taper_l * sa + (plate_h - tip_h) / 2 * ca
let top_open = jaw_y - tip_h / 2 * sa + (body_h / 2 - J.z - tip_h / 2 * ca) * edge_y / edge_z
let low_y    = jaw_y + tip_h / 2 * sa
let low_z    = J.z - tip_h / 2 * ca
let low_dy   = taper_l * ca + (plate_h - tip_h) / 2 * sa
let low_dz   = taper_l * sa - (plate_h - tip_h) / 2 * ca
let floor_t  = low_z + (cheek_back - low_y) * low_dz / low_dy + body_h / 2

// the number clip: one slim solid C that hugs the jaw, printed lying flat. A round pin, 0.4 mm smaller than the
// number's centre hole, runs through the near cheek, the number and the far cheek and on past it; a thin spine runs down
// the outside of the near cheek to a leg that runs back under the jaw's flat bottom, whose catch clicks up behind the
// far cheek's bottom edge; and a short tab hangs below the spine to pull the clip out by. The spine and the leg bend
// together as the catch rides under the jaw, so the deep catch strains them little. The catch face stands at 70°:
// latched, the jaw's edge sits on it with the leg still bent a little, which draws the head against the near cheek, and
// no pull on the clip's head and no knock on its pin opens it. Pressing the hook's sloped nose, past the far cheek, down frees
// the catch, and the clip then slides out by its tab
let pin_r    = pin_d / 2
let ph       = pin_d + 0.3                           // the pin's round hole through the jaw: 0.15 mm all round
let ph_roof  = 0.15                                  // the hole's top is a flat this far above the round, on 45° sides, so it prints clean and cannot sag onto the pin
let ph_s     = ph / 2 / sqrt(2)                      // where those sides leave the round
let ph_f     = 2 * ph_s - ph / 2 - ph_roof           // and half the flat's width
let pin_fl   = 0.3                                   // the pin flares by this where it leaves the head
let mouth    = 0.4                                   // and the hole's mouth at the near cheek is countersunk round the flare
let pin_c    = 0.8                                   // the pin's pointed tip
// the clip's flat side, on the bed: the pin is cut flat there, where its side reaches 45°, so it prints without support
let clip_y   = pivot_y - pin_r / sqrt(2)
let clip_w   = cheek_back - 0.25 - clip_y            // as wide as the jaw lets it be behind the pin
let pin_bot  = H_z - pin_r
let pin_top  = H_z + pin_r
let jaw_bot  = -body_h / 2
let sp_gap   = 1.2                                   // between the spine and the near cheek
let sp_t     = 2 + 0.03 * (body_h - 20)              // the spine: thicker where a taller jaw makes it longer
let leg_t    = 2                                     // the leg under the jaw
let sp_x     = jaw_w / 2 + sp_gap                    // the spine's inside face, which the leg rises from
let hx       = sp_x + sp_t                           // and its outside face
let head_top = pin_top + 1
let head_bot = pin_bot - 0.8                         // the head stops against the near cheek round the pin, from here up
let r_in     = 0.9                                   // the spine's root fillet under the head
let sp_len   = head_bot - r_in - jaw_bot             // the spine's thin length, from that fillet down to the leg
let clip_pre = 0.25                                  // latched, the catch stays bent this much on the jaw's edge, so the clip sits tight
let catch_d  = 1                                     // and holds this deep behind the far cheek
let catch_y  = clip_pre + catch_d                    // how far the catch rides down as it passes under the jaw
let clip_ret = 70                                    // the catch face's slope: steep enough that no pull opens it
let clip_in  = 40                                    // the hook's lead-in
// from the spine to the catch face's foot: latched, the far cheek's bottom edge sits on the face clip_pre above the leg
let leg_l    = sp_x + jaw_w / 2 - clip_pre / tan(clip_ret)
// the hook past the catch face's foot, as snap_hook draws it: the face's lean, its flat top, then its lead-in slope
let hook_back = catch_y / tan(clip_ret)
let hook_land = max(0.2, 0.25 * catch_y)
let hook_run = (catch_y + 0.6 * leg_t) / tan(clip_in)
// where the lead-in meets the jaw's bottom, latched: pushed in, it reaches the jaw's near edge there
let clip_lead = sp_x - leg_l - hook_back - hook_land - catch_y / tan(clip_in)
let nose_x   = sp_x - leg_l - hook_back - hook_land - hook_run    // the hook's tip
// the pin runs on just past the hook's tip, so a knock from that side lands on the pin, which cannot open the catch
let pin_far  = -jaw_w / 2 - nose_x + 0.2
let pin_tip  = -jaw_w / 2 - pin_far
let tab_l    = 4                                     // the pull tab below the leg
let tab_bot  = jaw_bot - leg_t - tab_l
let tab_r    = sp_t / 2                              // its rounded end
let k2       = 1 - 1 / sqrt(2)                       // where a 90° round's arc passes its corner, per mm of radius
// the head round the pin, the spine down the near cheek and the tab below the leg, as one outline (the leg is the hook)
let clip_sec = outline([(jaw_w / 2, head_bot), (sp_x - r_in, head_bot), arc_to((sp_x, head_bot - r_in), via: (sp_x - k2 * r_in, head_bot - k2 * r_in)), (sp_x, tab_bot + tab_r), arc_to((sp_x + tab_r, tab_bot), via: (sp_x + k2 * tab_r, tab_bot + k2 * tab_r)), arc_to((hx, tab_bot + tab_r), via: (hx - k2 * tab_r, tab_bot + k2 * tab_r)), (hx, head_top - 1.5), arc_to((hx - 1.5, head_top), via: (hx - 1.5 * k2, head_top - 1.5 * k2)), (jaw_w / 2 + 0.6, head_top), arc_to((jaw_w / 2, head_top - 0.6), via: (jaw_w / 2 + 0.6 * k2, head_top - 0.6 * k2))])
// the pin, turned about its own axis from inside the head: flared where it leaves the head, pointed at its tip
let pin_x0   = sp_x + sp_t / 2
// the catch's spring, as the springs check works it out (a C: the leg and the spine bend under one moment), for the
// requirements: its push in, its pull out, ASA on ASA (μ about 0.3, E about 1.9 GPa)
let clip_I   = clip_w * leg_t^3 / 12
let clip_Is  = clip_w * sp_t^3 / 12
let clip_a   = leg_l + sp_t / 2
let clip_k   = 1 / (leg_l^3 / (3 * 1900 * clip_I) + clip_a^2 * sp_len / (1900 * clip_Is))
let clip_P   = clip_k * catch_y
let clip_push = clip_P * (0.3 + tan(clip_in)) / (1 - 0.3 * tan(clip_in))
let clip_pull = clip_P * (0.3 + tan(clip_ret)) / (1 - 0.3 * tan(clip_ret))
// pressed down halfway along its lead-in, the hook bends the leg and the spine until the catch is 0.1 mm clear of the
// far cheek
let nose_d   = leg_l + hook_back + hook_land + hook_run / 2
let nose_N   = (catch_y + 0.1) / (leg_l^2 * (3 * nose_d - leg_l) / (6 * 1900 * clip_I) + clip_a * (nose_d + sp_t / 2) * sp_len / (1900 * clip_Is))
// a hard pull on the tab while the catch holds: the pull and the catch make a couple at the spine's foot, which the
// spine (held square at the head) and the leg (held at the catch) share by their stiffness. The pull that would start to
// bend the spine or the leg for good (ASA yields at about 34 MPa), as the springs check works it out
let tab_grip = leg_t / 2 + tab_l / 2                 // from the leg's middle to the tab's
let tab_share = (4 * sp_t^3 / sp_len) / (4 * sp_t^3 / sp_len + 3 * leg_t^3 / leg_l)
let tab_arm  = tab_grip + leg_t / 2
let tab_sp   = 6 * tab_share * tab_arm / (clip_w * sp_t^2)                                   // MPa in the spine per newton
let tab_leg  = 6 * (1 - tab_share) * tab_arm / (clip_w * leg_t^2) + 1 / (clip_w * leg_t)     // and in the leg, which the pull also stretches
let tab_tug  = 34 / max(tab_sp, tab_leg)
// a knock on the pin's tip from the far side: the catch holds the leg, the pin holds the head square and the leg bears
// on the jaw's near edge, poke_s from the spine's middle, so the spine bends in an S, about half as hard as a plain
// cantilever would. The push that would start to bend it for good
let poke_s   = sp_gap + sp_t / 2
let poke_kt  = leg_t^3 / poke_s * (4 - 4 / (4 + 3 * poke_s / jaw_w))
let poke_ks  = 2 * sp_t^3 / sp_len
let clip_poke = 34 * clip_w * sp_t^2 / (6 * sp_len) * (poke_ks + 2 * poke_kt) / (poke_ks + poke_kt)
// how far the clip stands out from the jaw: its spine at the near cheek, its pin and hook past the far one, its tab below
let clip_out = max(hx - jaw_w / 2, pin_far, tab_l)

part bracket {
  label: "Number bracket"
  color: #222428
  material: "ASA"
  group: "Bracket"
  rotate: (-tilt, 0, 0)
  // printed standing up as drawn: the block, the bosses on their necks and the jaw stand on the bed with square bottom
  // edges, and nothing hangs out
  body box(2 * bx, body_back, body_h, at: (0, body_back / 2, -body_h / 2)) as middle
  // each neck runs from the block's side back and out to its boss, with the groove round the boss's middle
  for side in [-1, 1] {
    add extrude(rotate(rect(neck_l, nw, at: (-side * neck_l / 2, 0)), side * neck_a), body_h, at: (side * px, py, -body_h / 2)) as necks
    add revolve(points: [(0, -body_h / 2), (rb - ch_bed, -body_h / 2), (rb, -body_h / 2 + ch_bed), (rb, zg1), (rb - gd, zg1 + 1.4 * gd), (rb - gd, -zg1 - 1.4 * gd), (rb, -zg1), (rb, body_h / 2), (0, body_h / 2)], at: (side * px, py, 0), dir: Z) as bosses
  }
  // the jaw: one solid block standing on the bed from the middle's back to past the centre hole, the full height
  add extrude(jaw_sec, jaw_w, at: (-jaw_w / 2, 0, 0), dir: X) as jaw
  // the seat's groove, top to bottom: every post wider than it bridges it and every round one nests in it
  cut extrude(polygon(points: [(-seat_a, -1), (-seat_a, 0), (-seat_f, seat_g), (seat_f, seat_g), (seat_a, 0), (seat_a, -1)]), body_h + 2, at: (0, 0, -body_h / 2 - 1)) as seat
  // the number's slot and the V pocket its point nests in; every edge of it faces up or back, so it prints without support
  cut extrude(pocket, slot, at: (-slot / 2, pivot_y, H_z), dir: X) as slot_cut
  // the pin's round hole through both cheeks, in line with the number's centre hole: its top is a short flat on 45°
  // sides (a bridge under 2 mm), so it prints clean with no support
  cut extrude(circle(ph, at: (pivot_y, H_z)) + polygon(points: [(pivot_y - ph_s, H_z + ph_s), (pivot_y + ph_s, H_z + ph_s), (pivot_y + ph_f, H_z + ph / 2 + ph_roof), (pivot_y - ph_f, H_z + ph / 2 + ph_roof)]), jaw_w + 2, at: (-jaw_w / 2 - 1, 0, 0), dir: X) as pin_hole
  // its mouth at the near cheek, countersunk round the pin's flare
  cut revolve(points: [(0, -0.5), (ph / 2 + mouth + 0.5, -0.5), (ph / 2, mouth), (0, mouth)], at: (jaw_w / 2, pivot_y, H_z), dir: -X) as pin_mouth
}

// the rubber a rear light's bracket has under it: a strip you cut from sheet (or an old inner tube, doubled) and stick
// into the seat, where it lines the face and the groove. Its post side, the seat's own outline rubber_t further out
let strip_o  = rubber_t * tan(seat_v / 2)
let strip_sec = polygon(points: [(-bx, 0), (-seat_a, 0), (-seat_f, seat_g), (seat_f, seat_g), (seat_a, 0), (bx, 0), (bx, -rubber_t), (seat_a - strip_o, -rubber_t), (seat_f - strip_o, seat_g - rubber_t), (-(seat_f - strip_o), seat_g - rubber_t), (-(seat_a - strip_o), -rubber_t), (-bx, -rubber_t)])
// what to cut: the seat's width measured along its face and down into the groove, by the bracket's height
let strip_w  = 2 * (bx - seat_a + (seat_a - seat_f) / cos(seat_v) + seat_f)

part strip {
  label: "Rubber strip"
  material: "EPDM"
  color: #2c2e33
  group: "Bracket"
  process: false
  rotate: (-tilt, 0, 0)
  held: "stuck into the bracket's seat with double-sided tape or a dab of superglue, and squeezed between the seat and the post by the O-ring"
  // cut with scissors from rubber sheet, strip_w wide and as tall as the bracket
  body extrude(strip_sec, body_h, at: (0, 0, -body_h / 2)) as liner
}

// ── The arms: a right and a left, each the other's mirror image ──
// both print in place with the bracket, standing on their bottom faces on the bed beside it, each turned about its boss
// to the middle of its swing (th_c), so its opening sits clear of the neck either side. The ring's underside and the
// channel's bottom flare slope at 55° and 50°, so nothing needs support. arm_r is on +x, which is the bike's left (seen
// from behind, +x is on your left), so the labels name the bike's side
part arm_r {
  label: "Arm (bike's left)"
  color: #222428
  material: "ASA"
  group: "Bracket"
  rotate: (atan2(sin(-tilt) * cos(th), cos(-tilt)), asin(-sin(-tilt) * sin(th)), atan2(cos(-tilt) * sin(th), cos(th)))
  at: (px, py * ct, -py * st)
  held: "printed in place round the bracket's boss: the ring inside its clip runs in the groove round the boss, so it cannot come off up or down, and its opening is narrower than the boss, so it cannot come off sideways; the O-ring's pull swings it in onto the post"
  body arm_body as arm
  fillet(1.5, edges: near((fx_t, yft, 0), 0.3))
  add knuckle as clip
  cut bore_s as bore
  add ring_s as keeper
  cut mouth_s as mouth_cut
  cut channel_s as channel
  add wall_s as outer_wall
  fillet(1, edges: near((fx_f, yfront, 0), 0.3) + near((xend, yfront, 0), 0.3))
}

part arm_l {
  label: "Arm (bike's right)"
  color: #222428
  material: "ASA"
  group: "Bracket"
  // the right arm's mirror image
  rotate: (atan2(sin(-tilt) * cos(th), cos(-tilt)), asin(sin(-tilt) * sin(th)), atan2(-cos(-tilt) * sin(th), cos(th)))
  at: (-px, py * ct, -py * st)
  held: "printed in place round the bracket's boss: the ring inside its clip runs in the groove round the boss, so it cannot come off up or down, and its opening is narrower than the boss, so it cannot come off sideways; the O-ring's pull swings it in onto the post"
  body mirror(arm_body, normal: X) as arm
  fillet(1.5, edges: near((-fx_t, yft, 0), 0.3))
  add mirror(knuckle, normal: X) as clip
  cut mirror(bore_s, normal: X) as bore
  add mirror(ring_s, normal: X) as keeper
  cut mirror(mouth_s, normal: X) as mouth_cut
  cut mirror(channel_s, normal: X) as channel
  add mirror(wall_s, normal: X) as outer_wall
  fillet(1, edges: near((-fx_f, yfront, 0), 0.3) + near((-xend, yfront, 0), 0.3))
}

// a patch of the same rubber on each finger, where it meets the post: it grips where bare plastic would slide, and it
// keeps the plastic off the post's paint
part grip_r {
  label: "Finger rubber (bike's left)"
  material: "EPDM"
  color: #2c2e33
  group: "Bracket"
  process: false
  rotate: (atan2(sin(-tilt) * cos(th), cos(-tilt)), asin(-sin(-tilt) * sin(th)), atan2(cos(-tilt) * sin(th), cos(th)))
  at: (px, py * ct, -py * st)
  held: "stuck onto the arm's finger with a dab of superglue, and pressed between the finger and the post by the O-ring"
  // cut with scissors from one layer of rubber, patch_w wide and as tall as the bracket
  body extrude(patch_sec, body_h, at: (0, 0, -body_h / 2)) as patch
}

part grip_l {
  label: "Finger rubber (bike's right)"
  material: "EPDM"
  color: #2c2e33
  group: "Bracket"
  process: false
  rotate: (atan2(sin(-tilt) * cos(th), cos(-tilt)), asin(sin(-tilt) * sin(th)), atan2(-cos(-tilt) * sin(th), cos(th)))
  at: (-px, py * ct, -py * st)
  held: "stuck onto the arm's finger with a dab of superglue, and pressed between the finger and the post by the O-ring"
  body mirror(extrude(patch_sec, body_h, at: (0, 0, -body_h / 2)), normal: X) as patch
}

// ── The number clip ─────────────────────────────────────
part number_clip {
  label: "Number clip"
  color: #2b2d32
  material: "ASA"
  group: "Bracket"
  process: mfg.fdm(build: Y)
  rotate: (-tilt, 0, 0)
  held: "clicks on: its pin goes through the jaw and the number, its leg runs under the jaw and its catch springs up behind the far cheek's bottom edge, and its head stops it at the near cheek; pressing the hook's nose past the far cheek down frees it"
  // printed lying on its flat side as drawn, so the spine and the leg bend along their layers and nothing hangs out: the
  // round pin is cut flat on that side where it reaches 45°. Print a spare
  body revolve(points: [(0, 0), (pin_r + pin_fl, 0), (pin_r + pin_fl, pin_x0 - jaw_w / 2), (pin_r, pin_x0 - jaw_w / 2 + pin_fl), (pin_r, pin_x0 - pin_tip - pin_c), (pin_r - pin_c, pin_x0 - pin_tip), (0, pin_x0 - pin_tip)], at: (pin_x0, pivot_y, H_z), dir: -X) as pin_bar
  cut box(hx - pin_tip + 2, 4, 2 * pin_r + 4, at: ((hx + pin_tip) / 2, clip_y - 2, H_z - pin_r - 2)) as pin_flat
  add extrude(clip_sec, clip_w, at: (0, clip_y + clip_w, 0), dir: -Y) as head
  add snap_hook(at: (sp_x, clip_y + clip_w / 2, jaw_bot - leg_t / 2), length: leg_l, t: leg_t, w: clip_w, catch: catch_y, dir: -X, facing: Z, angle: clip_in, retain: clip_ret, preload: clip_pre, root_r: 0.8, cycles: 200, frame: sp_len, frame_t: sp_t, grip: tab_grip) as clip_catch
}

// ── The race number, as the Race Result plate it is ─────
// each of its parts is drawn with the point at the origin, then tilted up by rise and moved to the jaw
let ink      = offset(text("004", size: 31, at: (4.4, 0)), 0.45) + offset(text("4", size: 14, at: (-29, -6.2)), 0.25)
let ink_at   = (0, 59.5, -17.5)
let sticker  = rect(105, 28, r: 2, at: (67, 12.5))
let stripe   = overlap(tag, rect(12, plate_h + 2, at: (plate_l - 6, 0)))

part number {
  label: "Race number (4004)"
  material: "PP"
  color: #f4f4f0
  group: "Number"
  process: false
  rotate: (rise, 0, 0)
  held: "nests in the jaw's V pocket by its point and is pinned through its centre hole by the number clip's pin"
  at: J_w
  body extrude(tag, plate_t, at: (-plate_t / 2, 0, 0), dir: X) as plate
  // the centre hole at the point, where the old bracket's bolt went and the locking pin now goes
  cut hole(hole_cd, through: true, at: (plate_t / 2, hole_c, 0), dir: -X)
  for zz in [-hole_z, hole_z] {
    cut hole(hole_d, through: true, at: (plate_t / 2, hole_in, zz), dir: -X)
  }
  // two more along its top edge, for brackets that tie it on
  for uu in [49, 70] {
    cut hole(hole_d, through: true, at: (plate_t / 2, uu, plate_h / 2 - 4), dir: -X)
  }
}

// printed on both faces; each reads from its own side
part number_print {
  label: "Race number print"
  material: "PP"
  color: #121214
  group: "Number"
  process: false
  rotate: (rise, 0, 0)
  at: J_w
  body emboss(ink, at: ink_at + (-plate_t / 2 - 0.05, 0, 0), facing: -X, h: 0.03)
  add emboss(ink, at: ink_at + (plate_t / 2 + 0.05, 0, 0), facing: X, h: 0.03)
}

part number_stripe {
  label: "Race number stripe"
  material: "PP"
  color: #f2c200
  group: "Number"
  process: false
  rotate: (rise, 0, 0)
  at: J_w
  body extrude(stripe, 0.05, at: (plate_t / 2, 0, 0), dir: X)
  add extrude(stripe - sticker, 0.05, at: (-plate_t / 2 - 0.05, 0, 0), dir: X)
}

// a foil antenna under clear film: silver-grey, not shiny metal
part transponder {
  label: "Timing transponder sticker"
  material: "PP"
  finish: "gloss"
  color: #c9cac6
  group: "Number"
  process: false
  rotate: (rise, 0, 0)
  at: J_w
  body extrude(sticker, 0.1, at: (-plate_t / 2 - 0.1, 0, 0), dir: X)
}

// ── The O-ring: one end in the left arm's channel, round the post's front high and low, its other end into the right
// arm's. The channels are on the arms, so from: and to: are where the arms put them, in the bracket's frame; each end slides
// into its channel from the arm's outer side (open:), over the top and under the bottom of the head
component oring = lib.band("EPDM", id: oring_id, cs: oring_cs, around: post, holds: bracket, hooked_on: [arm_l, arm_r], from: (-hRx, hRy, 0), to: (hRx, hRy, 0), pin: prong_d, lip: lip, spread: body_h - prong_d, open: [(-cos(th), sin(th), 0), (cos(th), sin(th), 0)], carries: 34g, shock: bump_g, saddle_mu: rubber_mu) {
  group: "Bands"
}

decision seatpost "Which seatpost" {
  ask: "Fit the bracket to any seatpost"
  chose: "Giant Propel"
  by: "you"
  why: "The bracket, its arms and the rubber are the same for every post: a flat back wider than the seat's 13 mm groove lies flat on the bracket's face, a round post or a narrow tail nests in the groove, and the arms swing in or out until their fingers rest on the post's sides. Each choice here only redraws the post and picks the metric 3.5 mm O-ring that stretches 40 to 46 % from arm to arm round the post's front: 44 × 3.5 for the Propel and D-shaped aero posts, 41 × 3.5 for 30.9 and 31.6 mm posts, 36 × 3.5 for 27.2 mm and 34 × 3.5 for 25.4 mm. The arms reach posts from about 17 to 35 mm wide."
  option "Giant Propel" { post_w: 20mm, post_d: 36mm, tail_w: 20mm, tail_r: 1.5mm, oring_id: 44mm, oring_cs: 3.5mm } "About 20 × 36 mm with a flat back (measure yours and set the sizes): a 44 × 3.5 mm O-ring."
  option "Round 27.2 mm" { post_w: 27.2mm, post_d: 27.2mm, tail_w: 0mm, tail_r: 0mm, oring_id: 36mm, oring_cs: 3.5mm } "The commonest round post: a 36 × 3.5 mm O-ring (a 37.5 × 3.5 goes on a little more easily)."
  option "Round 30.9 mm" { post_w: 30.9mm, post_d: 30.9mm, tail_w: 0mm, tail_r: 0mm, oring_id: 41mm, oring_cs: 3.5mm } "A wider round post: a 41 × 3.5 mm O-ring."
  option "Round 31.6 mm" { post_w: 31.6mm, post_d: 31.6mm, tail_w: 0mm, tail_r: 0mm, oring_id: 41mm, oring_cs: 3.5mm } "The widest common round post: a 41 × 3.5 mm O-ring."
  option "D-shaped aero (22 × 38 mm)" { post_w: 22mm, post_d: 38mm, tail_w: 18mm, tail_r: 2mm, oring_id: 44mm, oring_cs: 3.5mm } "A D-shaped aero post with an 18 mm flat back: a 44 × 3.5 mm O-ring."
}

decision holding "How it holds the post" {
  ask: "Hold the bracket on any seatpost without a screw, so it locks on and does not move around"
  chose: "Arms that swing, one O-ring in their channels"
  by: "you"
  why: "You asked to keep the two arms so it locks onto the post, with a better design. v15's solid bracket touched the post only at its seat, and on the Propel's flat back a sideways push of about 370 g on the number's tail was enough to start it rocking. With the arms, the O-ring's pull swings each one in until its finger grips the post's side, so the holder is held at three places, and both fingers must be dragged along the post before the bracket can turn or twist: about 590 g on the tail on the Propel, where the number itself folds over at about 310 g. Better than v14's arms: the O-ring pulls 6 mm outboard of each pivot instead of 3.4 and is stretched 40 % instead of 35, so each finger presses on with about 26 N instead of 20; each finger is faced with rubber, so it grips where bare plastic slid and keeps the plastic off the paint; and the block is v15's plain one with its flat seat on rubber you cut, so nothing is reprinted for another post. The arms print in place round their bosses, as v13 and v14's did, so they cannot come off. A closed band cannot get round a seatpost that carries on above and below, so one end of the O-ring goes into one arm's channel, wraps the post's front high and low and is stretched into the other arm's channel. Nothing held on by a rubber ring is as rigid as a bolted clamp: a deliberate push of about 460 g up or down on the tail still nods it, and it springs back."
  option "Arms that swing, one O-ring in their channels" "Held at the seat and by a finger each side; 47 mm wide on the Propel, printed in one go with the arms in place."
  option "One solid bracket, the O-ring in its shoulders" "v15: 30 mm wide and nothing moves, but only its seat touches the post, so it rocks more easily."
  option "Clamp with a screw" "Holds hardest, but needs a tool and can mark the post."
}

decision seat_grip "What grips the post" {
  ask: "Stop the bracket turning on the post with only one O-ring"
  chose: "Rubber you cut: a strip in the seat and a patch on each finger"
  by: "you"
  why: "You asked to drop the printed TPU pad and use rubber you cut to shape. Bare ASA on painted carbon grips at about μ 0.3; rubber about 0.5 even wet. A flat sheet cannot be shaped to each post the way the printed pad was, so the seat does that instead: it is the bracket's flat front face with a shallow groove down its middle, 12.8 mm wide and 1.8 mm deep with sides sloping 20°. A flat-backed post lies flat on the face either side of the groove, and a round post nests in the groove on its two sides, like a rod in a V block, so neither rocks, and one strip fits every post. Cut the strip 17 × 20 mm from rubber 1 to 3 mm thick, about 2 mm is best (an old inner tube, doubled), and stick it into the seat with double-sided tape or a dab of superglue. Cut two patches 7 × 20 mm from one layer of the same rubber (about 1 mm) and glue one round each arm's finger, where it meets the post: v14's fingers were bare plastic, which slides on paint and can mark it."
  option "Rubber you cut: a strip in the seat and a patch on each finger" "No print: 17 × 20 mm of rubber about 2 mm thick in the seat and two 7 × 20 mm patches about 1 mm thick on the fingers; the same for every post."
  option "TPU pad shaped to the post" "v10 to v14: sits on the post all over, but a second material and a new print for each post, and bare fingers."
}

decision number_hold "How the number is held" {
  ask: "Keep the number in without a screw"
  chose: "One-piece clip round the jaw"
  by: "claude"
  why: "You asked for a clip that is less in the way, still easy to pull out, with a round pin that fits the number's hole, and that does not come out unless you want it to. It still pins the number where the old bolt did, through the centre hole in its point, and the point still nests in its V pocket, so it cannot turn. The clip is a slim C that hugs the jaw: a round 4.8 mm pin that fills the number's 5.2 mm hole, a 2 mm spine 1.2 mm off the near cheek, a leg under the jaw's flat bottom and a 4 mm tab below it, with nothing standing more than 4 mm out. Its catch clicks up 1 mm behind the far cheek's bottom edge on a face so steep (70°) that no pull on the clip's head and no knock on its pin opens it. About 14 N pushes it in. To take it out, press the hook's sloped nose, past the far cheek, down with a fingertip (about 8 N) and pull the clip out by its tab. A catch shallow enough to pull straight off would feel loose again; a twist lock could be turned by a leg brushing it; a latch built into the jaw is too short to bend safely. A spare clip is under a gram."
  option "One-piece clip round the jaw" "Push it in until it clicks; press its nose down and pull it out by its tab."
  option "Snap pin through both cheeks" "v11's: a small push-in pin; pinch its two prong tips to push it out."
  option "Bolt through the centre hole" "What it had: needs a nut and a spanner."
  option "Zip tie through the centre hole" "No print, but cut off and replaced every race."
}

decision number_angle "How the number sits" {
  ask: "Angle the number"
  chose: "Tail up 15°"
  by: "you"
  why: "You asked for a less aggressive angle than v9's 30°. At 15° the number's top front corner swings less far toward the post, so its centre hole sits 16 mm behind the bracket's front face, and the pin sits 2 mm above the bracket's middle so the jaw stays thick under the flatter number."
  option "Tail up 15°" { rise: 15 } "What you asked for."
  option "Tail up 30°" { rise: 30, pivot_y: 24mm } "v9's tilt: the jaw grows 8 mm longer to keep the corner clear; reprint the bracket."
  option "Level" { rise: 0, body_h: 30mm } "Straight back, as the old bracket held it: the bracket grows to 30 mm tall so the pocket still has solid under the number's point. Flatter than about 14° needs that; reprint the bracket and the clip, and cut the rubber strip 30 mm tall."
}

decision tilt_lock "How the tilt is held" {
  ask: "Hold the number at its tilt with no play"
  chose: "Fixed V pocket"
  by: "you"
  why: "v8's click dial could be pushed up past its top click, and its spring snapped where it joined the saddle. You asked for one fixed tilt: the number's point nests in a V pocket of its own outline, cut through the solid jaw at that tilt, in a slot only 0.2 mm wider than the number, so it cannot tilt up or down and nothing in the bracket bends."
  option "Fixed V pocket" "One tilt and no moving parts: reprint to change it."
  option "Push-to-click dial" "v8: three tilts, but its spring and the slits round it left the saddle too thin."
}

decision oring_rubber "Which O-ring" {
  ask: "Find an O-ring or similar band that will work"
  chose: "Metric 3.5 mm EPDM O-ring"
  by: "claude"
  why: "No joined double band like yours is sold on its own in Australia (Garmin's Varia bands are sold out), so it is a plain metric O-ring with a 3.5 mm cord, sized to the post, whose two strands do what your band's two loops do. With the arms back it only goes from arm to arm round the post's front, so it is v14's size again: 44 × 3.5 mm for the Propel, stretched 40 % (about 35 N to hook on). v15's long 56.74 mm O-ring does not fit this version. EPDM lasts outdoors; RS Australia sells it in bags of 20. Autopro sells single nitrile ones ($12.99 for the 44 mm, $1.49 for the 36 and 41 mm), fine to start with but nitrile cracks in the sun. For a harder hold on the Propel fit a 41 × 3.5: it pulls about a fifth harder and takes about 41 N to hook on. A photo of your band, or its loop's length, would let it be designed round that instead."
  option "Metric 3.5 mm EPDM O-ring" "RS Australia, a bag of 20: 44 × 3.5 mm (stock no. 258-7581) for the Propel and D-shaped posts, 41 × 3.5 mm (258-7572) for 30.9 and 31.6 mm posts, 37.5 × 3.5 mm (258-7543) for 27.2 mm ones."
  option "Metric 3.5 mm nitrile O-ring" "Autopro, one at a time ($12.99 for the 44 mm, $1.49 for the 36 and 41 mm): swap it each season."
  option "Your double band" "Send a photo or its loop's length and the channels are sized to it."
}

// ── What each arm passes to the bracket ───────────────
// The O-ring pulls each arm's head toward the post (Fb). The arm turns freely on its boss, so the post must push back on
// its finger (along the contact's normal) just hard enough to balance that pull's turn about the boss; the boss then
// takes the sum of the two (Fp), which is what the bracket feels. All in the bracket's own frame (x across, y back)
let tpx      = oring.to_pull.x
let tpy      = oring.to_pull.y * ct - oring.to_pull.z * st
let tpz      = oring.to_pull.y * st + oring.to_pull.z * ct
let Fbx      = oring.pull * tpx
let Fby      = oring.pull * tpy
let Fbz      = oring.pull * tpz
let Mb       = (nRx - px) * Fby - (nRy - py) * Fbx
let Mf       = (fcx - px) * cny - (fcy - py) * cnx
let finger   = -Mb / Mf
let Fpx      = Fbx + finger * cnx
let Fpy      = Fby + finger * cny
let Fp       = hypot(Fpx, Fpy)
// the boss bears on the clip's wall on the side away from the pull: how far round that is from the opening's middle
// (it should be on solid wall, well clear of the opening's edges)
let bear_a   = acos(clamp(-(Fpx * cos(mouth_c + th) + Fpy * sin(mouth_c + th)) / Fp, -1, 1))
let lfpx     = oring.from_pull.x
let lfpy     = oring.from_pull.y * ct - oring.from_pull.z * st
let lfpz     = oring.from_pull.y * st + oring.from_pull.z * ct
let FLbx     = oring.pull * lfpx
let FLby     = oring.pull * lfpy
let MbL      = (px - nRx) * FLby - (nRy - py) * FLbx
let fingerL  = -MbL / ((px - fcx) * cny + (fcy - py) * cnx)
let FLpx     = FLbx - fingerL * cnx
let FLpy     = FLby + fingerL * cny
// in the world, at each boss
let boss_r   = (px, py * ct - body_h / 2 * st, -py * st - body_h / 2 * ct)
let boss_l   = (-px, py * ct - body_h / 2 * st, -py * st - body_h / 2 * ct)
let F_r      = (Fpx, Fpy * ct + Fbz * st, -Fpy * st + Fbz * ct)
let F_l      = (FLpx, FLpy * ct + oring.pull * lfpz * st, -FLpy * st + oring.pull * lfpz * ct)

// ── What it takes to move it on the post ──────────────
// Nothing bolts it on, so the O-ring's pull and the rubber's grip are all that hold it, and a hard enough push on the
// number's tail will move any band-on mount. These sums show how much, by hand, with the O-ring's pull as it is after a
// day stretched (EPDM keeps about 87 % of its first pull, and loses little more after that)
let aged     = 0.87
let N_seat   = -(Fpy + FLpy)                         // what presses the seat onto the post
// a finger sliding along the post is helped or hindered by its own grip: pushed forward it digs in, dragged back it
// eases off (Mt is the grip's turn about the boss, per newton: the contact is inboard of the boss)
let Mt       = (fcx - px) * cnx + (fcy - py) * cny
let C_push   = -Mb / (Mf + rubber_mu * Mt)
let C_drag   = -Mb / (Mf - rubber_mu * Mt)
// where the seat bears: a flat back on the face between the groove and its own corners (or the strip's edge), a round
// post or a narrow tail on the groove's two sides
let flat_seat = !round_post && kx0 >= seat_a
let seat_half = flat_seat ? min(kx0, bx) : (round_post ? Rn : kx0 + tail_r) * sin(seat_v)
let seat_mid = flat_seat ? (seat_a + seat_half) / 2 : seat_half
// a sideways knock on the tail turns the bracket about the post (yaw) and twists it on its seat (spin). To turn, the
// bracket must rock over one edge of its seat (a flat back's corner, or one side of the groove under a round post),
// against the O-ring's pull and with both fingers sliding along the post. On a round post it could also slide right
// round, against the rubber's grip in the groove, both fingers' grip and the O-ring's own grip on the post
let tail_y   = jaw_y + plate_l * ca
let tail_z   = J.z + plate_l * sa
let rock_y   = round_post ? ny + Rn * cos(seat_v) : ys
let yaw_rock = -2 * seat_half * Fby + (fcx - seat_half) * C_push * cny - (fcy - rock_y) * C_push * cnx - (fcx + seat_half) * C_drag * cny + (fcy - rock_y) * C_drag * cnx + rubber_mu * (((fcx - seat_half) * cnx + (fcy - rock_y) * cny) * C_push + ((fcx + seat_half) * cnx + (fcy - rock_y) * cny) * C_drag)
let yaw_slide = rubber_mu * N_seat * Rn / cos(seat_v) + rubber_mu * (C_push + C_drag) * Rn + oring.grip_moment
// how much of what it takes a sideways push of 1 N on the tail, or at the number's middle, uses up
let yaw_per_N = max((tail_y - rock_y) / (aged * yaw_rock), round_post ? (tail_y - ny) / (aged * yaw_slide) : 0)
let mid_per_N = max((jaw_y + plate_l / 2 * ca - rock_y) / (aged * yaw_rock), round_post ? (jaw_y + plate_l / 2 * ca - ny) / (aged * yaw_slide) : 0)
// to twist on its seat the rubber must slide on the post, round the seat's middle, and both fingers must slide up or
// down the post's sides
let spin_hold = aged * rubber_mu * (N_seat * hypot(seat_mid, body_h / 4) + (finger + fingerL) * abs(fcx))
// a pothole throws the number's weight (and the clip's, 11 g) up or down at the middle of the number, which tries to nod
// the bracket over its bottom or top edge. The O-ring's whole forward pull holds it down, through the seat and (on a
// round post) the fingers, each a line as tall as the bracket
let N_fwd    = -(Fby + FLby)
let nod_hold = aged * N_fwd * body_h / 2
let nod_load = 0.011 * 9.81 * bump_g * (jaw_y + plate_l / 2 * ca - ys)
// the wind's push on the number, at its middle
let gust_N   = 0.6125 * (gust / 1000)^2 * 1.2 * (plate_l * plate_h - taper_l * (plate_h - tip_h) / 2) / 1000000

// ── Checks ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// the seat: the bracket's front face and its groove. A wide cylinder zone along the post, set just deep enough to take
// in the groove's floor, kept to the seat's width and off the top and bottom faces
let seat_zr  = 30
let seat_zy  = seat_g + 0.3 - seat_zr
let seat_c   = (0, seat_zy * ct - (body_h - 3) / 2 * st, -seat_zy * st - (body_h - 3) / 2 * ct)
let seat_zd  = 2 * seat_zr
let seat_w   = (2 * bx - 1, 400, 400)
// where the number bears on the jaw: the cheeks either side of it and the pocket it nests in, over a disc in the middle
// of its face inside the jaw that stops short of the jaw's top and back faces; a zone along X starts at its at:
let grip_c   = (-(jaw_w / 2 + 0.3), 14.5 * ct + H_z * st, -14.5 * st + H_z * ct)
let grip_d   = 10

analysis "Crosswind gust" {
  parts: [bracket]
  // the bracket is only pressed on by the O-ring, through its arms and the rubber strip: it must stay pressed and not turn on the post
  fix: contact(zone(at: seat_c, d: seat_zd, axis: post_ax, length: body_h - 3, within: zone(at: (0, 0, 0), size: seat_w)), mu: rubber_mu, grip: [oring])
  // each arm hands the O-ring's pull, and the post's push on its finger, to its boss
  load: force(zone(at: boss_r, d: 2 * rb + 1, axis: post_ax, length: body_h), F_r)
  load: force(zone(at: boss_l, d: 2 * rb + 1, axis: post_ax, length: body_h), F_l)
  // the wind on the number comes into the jaw as a force and its moment
  load: wind(zone(at: grip_c, d: grip_d, axis: X, length: jaw_w + 0.6), gust, dir: X, area: plate_l * plate_h - taper_l * (plate_h - tip_h) / 2, at: J_w + num_dir * (plate_l / 2))
  carries: mass(zone(at: grip_c, d: grip_d, axis: X, length: jaw_w + 0.6), 11g)
  gravity
  printed
  safety: 2
}

// a knock bends the thin number over before it can pass much force on: at most its bending strength where it leaves the
// jaw, cheek_back - pivot_y behind its centre hole
let number_bend = 30 * (plate_h * plate_t * plate_t / 6)          // N·mm, 30 MPa polypropylene
let knock_felt  = min(knock, number_bend / (plate_l - hole_c - (cheek_back - pivot_y)))

// the fingers' rubber resists the knock too: each can hold rubber_mu times its press along the post before it slides.
// The knock study gives each finger two thirds of that, with the O-ring a day old (or what cancels the knock's whole
// turn, if that is less), pushing back on the side the knock swings forward and forward on the other; the seat must
// hold the rest
let hold_f   = min(2 / 3 * rubber_mu * aged * min(finger, fingerL), knock_felt * tail_y / (2 * (fcx * cnx + fcy * cny)))
let hold_r   = (-hold_f * cny, hold_f * cnx * ct, -hold_f * cnx * st)
let hold_l   = (-hold_f * cny, -hold_f * cnx * ct, hold_f * cnx * st)
let hold_at_r = (fcx, fcy * ct, -fcy * st)
let hold_at_l = (-fcx, fcy * ct, -fcy * st)

analysis "Leg knock" {
  parts: [bracket]
  fix: contact(zone(at: seat_c, d: seat_zd, axis: post_ax, length: body_h - 3, within: zone(at: (0, 0, 0), size: seat_w)), mu: rubber_mu, grip: [oring])
  load: force(zone(at: boss_r, d: 2 * rb + 1, axis: post_ax, length: body_h), F_r)
  load: force(zone(at: boss_l, d: 2 * rb + 1, axis: post_ax, length: body_h), F_l)
  // and the fingers' grip on the post, which each arm also hands to its boss
  load: force(zone(at: boss_r, d: 2 * rb + 1, axis: post_ax, length: body_h), hold_r, at: hold_at_r)
  load: force(zone(at: boss_l, d: 2 * rb + 1, axis: post_ax, length: body_h), hold_l, at: hold_at_l)
  load: force(zone(at: grip_c, d: grip_d, axis: X, length: jaw_w + 0.6), knock_felt, dir: X, at: J_w + num_dir * plate_l)
  printed
  cycles: 1000
  safety: 2
}

analysis "Pothole" {
  parts: [bracket]
  fix: contact(zone(at: seat_c, d: seat_zd, axis: post_ax, length: body_h - 3, within: zone(at: (0, 0, 0), size: seat_w)), mu: rubber_mu, grip: [oring])
  load: force(zone(at: boss_r, d: 2 * rb + 1, axis: post_ax, length: body_h), F_r)
  load: force(zone(at: boss_l, d: 2 * rb + 1, axis: post_ax, length: body_h), F_l)
  carries: mass(zone(at: grip_c, d: grip_d, axis: X, length: jaw_w + 0.6), 11g)
  accel: (0, 0, -bump_g)
  printed
  safety: 2
}

// the bosses pull the middle forward at its sides and the post pushes back on the seat: at worst all of it at the middle,
// which bends the middle like a beam between its necks (its thinnest bit is behind the groove)
let body_mpa = 6 * max(abs(Fpy), abs(FLpy)) * px / (body_h * (body_back - seat_g)^2)
// each neck is a short cantilever from the block's side to its boss, bent by the boss's load across it
let neck_root = (px - bx) / cos(neck_a)
let neck_mpa = 6 * Fp * neck_root / (body_h * nw^2)
// each arm is bent where its body leaves the clip, across the body ro + 0.5 in front of the pivot, by the O-ring's pull
// and the post's push on the finger, and pulled along by them too
let y_rt     = -(ro + 0.5)
let x_in     = ro * cos(aB) + (y_rt - ro * sin(aB)) * (fx_t - ro * cos(aB)) / (yft - ro * sin(aB))
let root_w   = xb - x_in
let r0x      = px + (x_in + xb) / 2 * cos(th) - y_rt * sin(th)
let r0y      = py + (x_in + xb) / 2 * sin(th) + y_rt * cos(th)
let root_M   = abs((nRx - r0x) * Fby - (nRy - r0y) * Fbx + finger * ((fcx - r0x) * cny - (fcy - r0y) * cnx))
let root_mpa = 6 * root_M / (body_h * root_w^2) + Fp / (body_h * root_w)
// printed in place, the arm's clip keeps this much room from its boss's neck at the middle of its swing: at the opening's
// edges and at the ring's ends, each the closest point to the neck's side. And at either end of its swing the ring's ends
// still clear the neck, so the opening's edge is what stops it
let mouth_gap = ri * sin(mouth_h) - nw / 2
let ring_gap_c = ring_r * sin(ring_h) - nw / 2
let ring_gap_e = ring_r * sin(ring_h - swing) - nw / 2
// in use the O-ring's pull swings each arm (the strands leave the channel in the arm's own frame this far in from
// straight back out of it), and keeps its end deep in the channel
let pull_in  = -(-oring.to_pull.x * sin(th) + (oring.to_pull.y * ct - oring.to_pull.z * st) * cos(th)) / hypot(oring.to_pull.x, oring.to_pull.y * ct - oring.to_pull.z * st)

require "The round pin fills the number's centre hole: 0.3 to 0.6 mm smaller, so it goes through and the number cannot rattle on it" for fit, knock: hole_cd - pin_d >= 0.3 && hole_cd - pin_d <= 0.6
require "The number clip's pin is in its hole before the catch meets the jaw, so it goes in straight" for fit: clip_lead - pin_tip >= 0.5
require "Pushing the number clip in is easy: under 15 N" soft for fit: clip_push <= 15N
require "The number clip stays in until its nose is pressed: its catch takes over 50 N to pull past" for fit, bumps, knock: clip_pull >= 50N
require "A hard pull on the number clip's tab does not bend it: its spine takes at least 30 N" for fit: tab_tug >= 30N
require "A knock on the pin's tip does not bend the number clip: its spine takes at least 20 N" for knock: clip_poke >= 20N
require "Pressing the number clip's nose down frees its catch with a light press: under 10 N" soft for fit: nose_N <= 10N
require "The number clip is slim: nothing on it stands more than 4 mm out from the jaw" soft for fit: clip_out <= 4
require "The cheeks keep at least 1.9 mm of wall behind the pin's hole and 2.5 mm above it" for knock: cheek_back - pivot_y - ph / 2 >= 1.9 && body_h / 2 - H_z - ph / 2 - ph_roof >= 2.5
require "At its tilt, the number's top front corner stays clear of the seatpost" for fit: corner_y - ys >= 2
require "The pocket holds the number's point: solid runs on 4 mm in front of its upper edge and 2.5 mm under its lower one" for knock: top_open - seat_g >= 4 && floor_t >= 2.5
require "The number is tight in its slot: at most 0.3 mm of room" soft for knock: fit_gap <= 0.3
require "The middle is solid behind its groove, at least 5 mm thick" for gust, bumps, fit: body_back - seat_g >= 5
require "The middle takes the arms' pull three times over" for gust, bumps, fit: 34 / body_mpa >= 3
require "Each neck takes its arm's pull three times over" for gust, bumps, fit: 34 / neck_mpa >= 3
require "Each arm takes the O-ring's pull three times over where it leaves its clip" for gust, bumps, fit: 34 / root_mpa >= 3
require "Each arm rests on the post, not at the end of its swing" for fit: abs(th_free - th_c) <= swing - 2
require "The O-ring's pull presses each arm's finger onto the post, with at least a quarter of its own pull" for fit, gust, knock: finger >= 0.25 * oring.pull && fingerL >= 0.25 * oring.pull
require "Each finger meets the post on its rubber, at least 1 mm from the patch's ends" for fit: psi >= psi_lo && psi <= psi_hi
require "The pull presses each boss on its clip's solid wall, at least 15° clear of the opening" for fit, gust, bumps: bear_a >= mouth_h + 15
require "A clip cannot come off its boss sideways: its opening is at least 1 mm narrower than the boss" for fit, gust, bumps: mouth_w <= 2 * rb - 1
require "The arms print in place: at least 0.4 mm of room between each arm and the bracket as printed" for fit: min(clr, relief, mouth_gap, ring_gap_c) >= 0.4
require "An arm cannot be pulled off its boss: the ring inside its clip reaches at least 0.6 mm inside the boss's edge" for fit, gust, bumps, knock: gd - clr >= 0.6
require "At either end of an arm's swing the ring inside its clip still clears the neck" for fit: ring_gap_e >= 0.1
require "Each neck meets the block's side behind its front face, so nothing reaches into the seat" for fit: py - (px - bx) * tan(neck_a) - nw / 2 / cos(neck_a) >= 0.1
require "A round post nests in the groove on both its sides and stays off its floor" for fit, gust: round_post ? ny_round + R1 <= seat_g - 0.2 : true
require "The rubber strip is thick enough to grip and thin enough not to squirm: 1 to 3 mm" for fit, gust: rubber_t >= 1 && rubber_t <= 3
require "A sideways knock folds the number over before it turns the bracket on the post, at least one and a half times over with the O-ring a day old" for knock: knock_felt * yaw_per_N <= 0.667
require "A sideways knock folds the number over before it twists the bracket on its seat, at least one and a half times over" for knock: spin_hold / (knock_felt * tail_z) >= 1.5
require "A crosswind gust uses under a third of what it takes to turn the bracket on the post" for gust: gust_N * mid_per_N <= 0.34
require "A pothole uses under a third of what it takes to nod the bracket on the post" for bumps: nod_load / nod_hold <= 0.34
require "The O-ring pulls firmly" soft for gust, bumps: oring.stretch >= 1.3
require "Stretching the O-ring into its channel is a one-hand job" soft for fit: oring.fit_force <= 45N
require "Each end of the O-ring sits at least three quarters of a cord deep in its channel, so a knock cannot flick it out" for fit, bumps: lip >= 0.75 * oring_cs
require "The O-ring's pull keeps each end in its channel: within 60° of straight into it" for fit, gust: pull_in >= 0.5

test "Fit and remove": "The arms come off the printer on their bosses: turn each one firmly in and out once to break it free. It should then swing freely and not come off up, down or sideways. Stick the rubber strip into the bracket's seat, pressed down into its groove, and glue a rubber patch round each arm's finger. Hold the bracket on the post: a flat back should lie flat on the rubber and a round post should nest in the groove without rocking. Loop one end of the O-ring over the top and under the bottom of the left arm's head and slide it in from the side until it drops into the channel in the back of the head; wrap it round the post's front high and low and do the same with the other end on the right arm. Both arms should swing in until their fingers' rubber rests on the post's sides. Slide the number's point into the jaw's slot, tail up, until it sits in its pocket and its centre hole lines up with the round hole; push the number clip's pin in through the near cheek, with your thumb on the clip over the pin, until the catch on its leg clicks up behind the far cheek's bottom edge. It should not rattle, and a push on the pin's tip from the far side should not move it. Press the hook's sloped nose, past the far cheek, down with a fingertip and pull the clip out by the tab under the jaw: it should slide out with almost no force. To take it all off, take the clip out, slide the number out, then unhook the O-ring."
test "Knock": "Push the number's tail sideways by hand until the number bends over: the bracket must not turn or twist on the post. Push the tail up and down: the number must not tilt or rattle in the jaw, and the bracket should only start to nod with a firm push (about 460 g on the Propel) and spring straight back."
test "Shake test": "Ride 10 minutes of rough road and a 50 km/h descent; the bracket must not turn on the post, the arms must stay on their bosses with their fingers on the post, and the number must not move in the jaw."
buy "EPDM O-rings, 3.5 mm cord, a bag of 20 in the size the seatpost choice gives: 44 × 3.5 mm for the Propel (plus $20 delivery under $80 ex GST)" { qty: 1, price: 29.25, url: "https://au.rs-online.com/web/p/gaskets-o-rings/2587581" }
